## Comprehensive Concepts with Theory & Examples <a name="concepts"></a>

### Concept 1: Record Types - The Foundation of Data Management

#### Theory
A Record Type is Appian's primary data modeling construct that represents an entity in your business domain. It combines three key elements:
- **Data Storage**: Defines what information is stored
- **Business Logic**: Rules that govern data behavior
- **User Interaction**: How data is viewed and edited

#### Key Principles
```
Record Type = Database Table + Validation + Business Rules + UI Views
```

#### Core Components

**1. Fields - The Basic Unit**
```
Field Definition:
- Name: Unique identifier within record
- Type: Data type (Text, Number, Date, etc.)
- Properties: Required, searchable, sortable
- Validation: Rules and constraints

Field Types Explained:

1. Text Field
   Purpose: Store string data
   Use Cases: Names, emails, addresses
   Example:
     firstName: "John"
     email: "john@company.com"

2. Number Field
   Purpose: Store numeric values
   Use Cases: Quantities, scores, measurements
   Properties: Precision, scale, min/max
   Example:
     quantity: 100
     price: 49.99
     discount: 10

3. Date/Time Field
   Purpose: Store temporal data
   Types:
     - Date: Only date (2024-12-31)
     - Time: Only time (14:30:00)
     - DateTime: Both (2024-12-31 14:30:00)
     - Timestamp: DateTime with timezone
   Example:
     createdDate: 2024-01-15
     lastModified: 2024-12-31 14:30:00 EST

4. Boolean Field
   Purpose: True/false values
   Use Cases: Flags, toggles, active status
   Example:
     isActive: true
     isVerified: false

5. Dropdown Field
   Purpose: Single selection from predefined list
   Implementation:
     - Static options: defined in field properties
     - Dynamic options: from expression rule
   Example:
     status: "Active" (options: Active, Inactive, Pending)
     department: "Sales"

6. Multi-select Field
   Purpose: Multiple selections from list
   Use Cases: Tags, permissions, assignments
   Example:
     skills: ["Java", "Python", "JavaScript"]
     roles: ["Reviewer", "Approver"]

7. User Field
   Purpose: Reference to system users
   Benefits: Auto-complete, avatars, profile links
   Example:
     assignedTo: [John Doe - john@company.com]
     manager: [Jane Smith - jane@company.com]

8. Link Field
   Purpose: Reference to other record types
   Creates: Relationship between records
   Example:
     employeeID → Employee record
     departmentID → Department record

9. Document Field
   Purpose: Store file attachments
   Use Cases: Resumes, contracts, receipts
   Example:
     supportingDocuments: [doc1.pdf, doc2.docx]
     receipt: invoice.pdf

10. Structured Type Field
    Purpose: Nested objects with multiple properties
    Use Cases: Complex data structures
    Example:
      address: {
        street: "123 Main St",
        city: "New York",
        zipCode: "10001"
      }
```

**2. Relationships Between Records**

```
Type 1: One-to-Many (Most Common)
Theory:
- Parent record can have multiple children
- Child belongs to one parent
- Foreign key stored in child
Example:
  Department (1) ←→ Employee (Many)
  - One department has many employees
  - Each employee belongs to one department
  Implementation:
    Department Record:
      - deptID: "DEPT-001"
      - deptName: "Sales"
    Employee Record:
      - empID: "EMP-001"
      - name: "John"
      - department: [Link to Department DEPT-001]

Type 2: Many-to-Many
Theory:
- Records on both sides can relate to multiple on the other side
- Typically requires junction table
Example:
  Student (Many) ←→ Course (Many)
  - Students enroll in multiple courses
  - Courses have multiple students
  Implementation:
    Student-Course Junction Record:
      - studentID: [Link]
      - courseID: [Link]
      - enrollmentDate: 2024-01-15
      - grade: "A"

Type 3: One-to-One
Theory:
- Each record on one side relates to exactly one on the other
- Rare in practice
Example:
  Employee (1) ←→ EmployeeProfile (1)
  - One employee has one profile
  - One profile belongs to one employee
  Implementation:
    Both records have links to each other
```

#### Complete Record Type Example

```
Record Type: Project

Field Structure:
{
  projectID: Text (Primary Key),
    └─ Generated: "PROJ-" & counter()
    
  projectName: Text (Required),
    └─ Validation: length < 255, not empty
    
  description: Text,
    └─ Multiline text, allow rich formatting
    
  status: Dropdown (Required),
    └─ Options: [Planning, Active, On Hold, Completed, Archived]
    └─ Default: Planning
    
  startDate: Date (Required),
    └─ Validation: Cannot be in past
    
  endDate: Date,
    └─ Validation: Must be after startDate
    
  budget: Number,
    └─ Format: Currency with 2 decimals
    └─ Validation: Must be > 0
    
  budget_spent: Number,
    └─ Auto-calculated from expenses
    └─ Formula: SUM(expenses.amount)
    
  budget_remaining: Number,
    └─ Calculated field: budget - budget_spent
    
  manager: User Field (Required),
    └─ References system user
    └─ Used for assignment and notifications
    
  team_members: Multi-select User Field,
    └─ Multiple users can be assigned
    └─ Tracked for collaboration
    
  department: Link to Department Record,
    └─ Many-to-One relationship
    └─ Used for rollup reporting
    
  attachments: Document Field,
    └─ Stores multiple documents
    └─ Project plans, specifications, etc.
    
  createdDate: DateTime (System field),
    └─ Auto-set when record created
    
  createdBy: User Field (System field),
    └─ Auto-set to current user
    
  lastModifiedDate: DateTime (System field),
    └─ Auto-updated on changes
    
  lastModifiedBy: User Field (System field),
    └─ Auto-updated to current user
}
```

#### Record Views - Different Perspectives

**Purpose**: Display record data in different formats for different use cases

```
View Types:

1. Summary View
   Purpose: Show most important fields
   Used in: Record detail page, dashboards
   Fields: projectName, status, manager, budget_remaining
   Layout: Cards or summary panels

2. All Fields View
   Purpose: Show complete record information
   Used in: Admin, editing
   Fields: All fields displayed
   Layout: Organized sections

3. Related Records View
   Purpose: Show child records
   Used in: Viewing team members, tasks
   Example:
     - Expenses related to project
     - Tasks in project
     - Team assignments

4. Filtered View
   Purpose: Query subset based on criteria
   Used in: Status-specific views
   Examples:
     - Active Projects Only
     - Projects by Manager
     - Overbudget Projects
   Query Filter:
     status = "Active" AND budget_spent > budget

5. Report View
   Purpose: Aggregated data and analytics
   Used in: Dashboards, reporting
   Shows: Counts, sums, trends
```

#### Record Rules - Business Logic

```
Rule Category 1: Validation Rules
Purpose: Ensure data integrity
Executed: When record is saved
Example:
  Rule: Validate Project Dates
  if(endDate < startDate,
    "End date must be after start date",
    null
  )

Rule Category 2: Auto-Population Rules
Purpose: Set default or calculated values
Triggered: On field change or save
Example:
  Rule: Calculate Remaining Budget
  if(isnull(budget_spent), budget, budget - budget_spent)

Rule Category 3: Uniqueness Rules
Purpose: Prevent duplicate records
Example:
  Rule: Project Name Uniqueness
  Ensure projectName is unique within department

Rule Category 4: Cross-field Rules
Purpose: Validate relationships between fields
Example:
  Rule: Budget Must Match
  if(status = "Approved", 
    budget > 0,
    true
  )
```

