### Concept 4: User Interfaces - SAIL (Simple Application Integration Language)

#### Theory
SAIL is Appian's expression language for building user interfaces. It's declarative (define WHAT, not HOW) and combines layout, data binding, and logic.

#### SAIL Architecture

```
Component Hierarchy:

a!formLayout [Top-level container]
  ├─ a!sectionLayout [Logical grouping]
  │  ├─ a!columnsLayout [Multi-column layout]
  │  │  ├─ a!columnLayout
  │  │  │  ├─ a!textField
  │  │  │  ├─ a!dropdown
  │  │  │  └─ a!buttonWidget
  │  │  └─ a!columnLayout
  │  │     ├─ a!dateField
  │  │     └─ a!textDisplayField
  │  ├─ a!cardLayout [Emphasized container]
  │  └─ a!divider [Visual separator]
  └─ a!buttonLayout [Action buttons]
```

#### Component Types with Examples

**1. Input Components**

```
Text Field:
a!textField(
  label: "Full Name",
  value: ri!employee.name,
  saveInto: ri!employee.name,
  required: true,
  disabled: ri!isReadOnly,
  placeholder: "Enter full name",
  validations: if(
    len(ri!employee.name) < 3,
    "Name must be at least 3 characters",
    {}
  ),
  helpText: "Enter your full legal name"
)

Theory:
- value: Current displayed value
- saveInto: Where to save when form submits
- required: Form cannot submit without value
- disabled: Gray out field, prevent editing
- validations: Real-time validation messages
- helpText: Contextual help

Number Field:
a!numberField(
  label: "Salary",
  value: ri!employee.salary,
  saveInto: ri!employee.salary,
  required: true,
  minValue: 0,
  maxValue: 1000000,
  decimals: 2,
  align: "RIGHT"
)

Theory:
- decimals: How many decimal places
- align: Right-align for numbers (standard)
- minValue/maxValue: Enforce range

Dropdown Field:
a!dropdownField(
  label: "Department",
  value: ri!employee.department,
  saveInto: ri!employee.department,
  required: true,
  choiceLabels: {"Sales", "IT", "HR", "Finance"},
  choiceValues: {"SALES", "IT", "HR", "FIN"},
  placeholderLabel: "-- Select Department --"
)

Theory:
- choiceLabels: What user sees
- choiceValues: What is saved
- placeholderLabel: Default option

Multi-select Dropdown:
a!multipleDropdownField(
  label: "Assign Reviewers",
  value: ri!reviewers,
  saveInto: ri!reviewers,
  choiceLabels: {"John", "Jane", "Bob"},
  choiceValues: {"john@co", "jane@co", "bob@co"},
  required: true
)

Theory:
- Returns array of selected values
- Allows multiple selections
- Useful for many-to-many relationships

Date Field:
a!dateField(
  label: "Birth Date",
  value: ri!employee.birthDate,
  saveInto: ri!employee.birthDate,
  required: true,
  disabledDates: today() - 1,
  minDate: today() - 36500,
  maxDate: today() - 6570
)

Theory:
- disabledDates: Cannot select certain dates
- minDate/maxDate: Date range constraints
- Useful for validating reasonable dates
```

**2. Display Components**

```
Text Display Field:
a!textDisplayField(
  label: "Employee ID",
  value: ri!employee.employeeID
)

Theory: Read-only display of data
- No input capability
- Used for viewing only
- Useful for calculated/reference fields

Rich Text Display:
a!richTextDisplayField(
  value: {
    "Status: ",
    a!richTextItem(
      text: ri!status,
      color: if(ri!status = "Approved", "GREEN", "RED"),
      style: "STRONG"
    )
  }
)

Theory:
- Multiple rich text items combined
- Color coding for visual feedback
- Styling (STRONG, EMPHASIS, etc.)

Card Layout:
a!cardLayout(
  contents: {
    a!columnsLayout(
      columns: {
        a!columnLayout(
          contents: { /* content */ }
        )
      }
    )
  },
  style: "ACCENT",
  marginAbove: "STANDARD"
)

Theory:
- Emphasizes important content
- Styled container
- Improves visual hierarchy
```

**3. Layout Components**

```
Columns Layout:
a!columnsLayout(
  columns: {
    a!columnLayout(
      width: "WIDE",
      contents: {
        a!textField(label: "Name", value: ri!name)
      }
    ),
    a!columnLayout(
      width: "NARROW",
      contents: {
        a!textDisplayField(label: "ID", value: ri!id)
      }
    )
  },
  spacing: "STANDARD"
)

Theory:
- Responsive column layout
- Width options: NARROW, MEDIUM, WIDE
- Automatic wrapping on small screens
- Spacing: Control gap between columns

Sections:
a!sectionLayout(
  label: "Personal Information",
  contents: {
    a!textField(label: "First Name", value: ri!firstName),
    a!textField(label: "Last Name", value: ri!lastName)
  },
  isCollapsible: true,
  isInitiallyCollapsed: false
)

Theory:
- Groups related fields
- Optional collapse/expand
- Visual organization
- Cleaner UI for long forms

Tabs:
a!tabContainer(
  tabs: {
    a!tabPane(
      label: "Basic Info",
      contents: { /* basic fields */ }
    ),
    a!tabPane(
      label: "Advanced",
      contents: { /* advanced fields */ }
    )
  }
)

Theory:
- Multiple panes in one form
- Reduce cognitive load
- Better organization
- Improved user experience
```

#### Conditional Logic in SAIL

```
Example 1: Show/Hide Fields
a!if(
  ri!employee.employmentType = "Contract",
  a!dateField(
    label: "Contract End Date",
    value: ri!employee.contractEndDate
  ),
  a!nullAs(null)
)

Theory:
- a!if returns component or null
- Null = field doesn't render
- Conditional visibility based on values

Example 2: Conditional Styling
a!richTextDisplayField(
  value: {
    a!richTextItem(
      text: "Status: " & ri!status,
      color: switch(
        ri!status,
        "Approved", "GREEN",
        "Rejected", "RED",
        "Pending", "ORANGE",
        "STANDARD"
      )
    )
  }
)

Theory:
- switch statement for multiple conditions
- Color maps to status
- Visual feedback for users

Example 3: Dynamic Form Layout
a!if(
  ri!userRole = "Manager",
  a!sectionLayout(
    label: "Manager Controls",
    contents: { /* manager-specific fields */ }
  ),
  a!nullAs(null)
)

Theory:
- Different forms for different roles
- Role-based access control at UI level
- Simpler interface for non-managers
```

